e":"PubMed","id":"8755878"}],"value":"Oxidoreductase that catalyzes the reduction of a variety of nitroaromatic compounds using NADPH as source of reducing equivalents (PubMed:34142705, PubMed:8755878). It is the major oxygen-insensitive nitroreductase in E.coli (PubMed:8755878). Reduces nitrofurazone by a ping-pong bi-bi mechanism possibly to generate a two-electron transfer product (PubMed:8755878). It is 20-50 fold more active with NADPH than with NADH (PubMed:35648111). The enzyme can also reduce quinones, such as 1,4-benzoquinone or menadione (PubMed:34142705, PubMed:8755878). It shows a weak FMN reductase activity (PubMed:8755878). It also has chromate (Cr(VI)) reductase activity, reducing chromate to the less soluble and less toxic Cr(III), and giving rise to a flavin semiquinone form of NfsA and Cr(V) as intermediates (PubMed:15250887). This leads to the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) (PubMed:15250887)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"34142705"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"35648111"}],"value":"NfsA can be used as prodrug activator (PubMed:34142705, PubMed:35648111). It can reduce and activate the synthetic antibiotics nitrofurazone and nitrofurantoin (PubMed:34142705, PubMed:35648111). It can catalyze the reduction of the anti-tumor prodrug CB1954 (5-(aziridin-1-yl)-2,4-dinitrobenzamide), leading to the formation of its cytotoxic derivative (PubMed:34142705, PubMed:35648111)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"a quinone + NADPH + H(+) = a quinol + NADP(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:46164"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:24646"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57783"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:58349"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:132124"}],"ecNumber":"1.6.5.10","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed"